ess sadness. [1366] \"It's quite normal for her to harbor a desire for revenge.\" [1367] Mukai's murmur was terrifying. I'm not sure if he intended to say it to me or if it was just an unintentional slip of the tongue. [1368] However, I didn't try to refute it and instead changed the subject by asking, \"So, what happened this morning? Even a simple greeting would have been nice.\" [1369] \"No, no, I saw that you were dreaming quite happily, so I didn't want to wake you up.\" [1370] Mukai made up a few lies, showing no intention of seriously explaining himself. Well, that's just how he is. [1371] \"Did you have any luck?\" [1372] \"You don't seem convinced.\" Mutsuzaki persisted with his story. [1373] \"Oh well.\" [1374] A gust of wind blew by, and a blue hat floated in front of us. A deep blue, like the sea. Was it due to the turbulent air currents? The straw hat that seemed to be lost drifted aimlessly in the courtyard. [1375] \"Whose hat is that?\" [1376] \"\u2026How would I know?\" [1377] Soon, the blue faded into the green of the plane trees. [1378] \"Isn't it yours? The color goes well with the red thread you use for your game of picking the beans.\" [1379] \"The base color of Kurogane City is red, black, and gray,\" Woodjutsu chuckled. [1380] The word \"gray\" was emphasized. Upon closer inspection, the people of the Minagorou family all had a gray appearance \u2013 not a dull gray, but a nearly lustrous silver-gray. [1381] It is said that silverware can distinguish poison, but the \"silver\" in Inaba contains a deadly amount of mercury. Unfortunately, I don't know which body it was mixed into. This is what Mochizuki referred to as \"gray.\" [1382] \"Also, Ariadne has a red ball of yarn.\" [1383] After noon, in order to investigate the existence of escape routes in detail, the police chief and his group searched \"Hell's Gate\" again, as if they were going to do a thorough sweep. [1384] It is not uncommon for ancient buildings like H\u014dy\u014d to have one or two hidden rooms or secret passages. However, like Mochizuki, I didn't have high expectations, and I just stood behind the marble door as an observer, watching the police chief and his group frantically searching. [1385] Mochizuki muttered, \"It's all wasted effort,\" as if watching a mediocre shogi game. [1386] \"There's not even a place where a mouse can fit in.\" [1387] As the western sky was dyed red, a burly detective with a beard made the above conclusion in a helpless and somewhat theatrical manner. [1388] \"What's going on here!\" [1389] Juzu leaned against the guillotine and said he had done his best. The \"Hell Gate\" being a room is fine, but the situation being the same as what Mutsu said is what makes him more upset. [1390] Before the chief of police, who was in a bad mood, could take out his anger on someone, we quickly retreated to our own rooms. [1391] \"Jiroku seems really fixated on that room,\" I said after catching my breath on the third floor. [1392] \"Being fixated isn't wrong, but his approach is off,\" Kusaka sniffed. [1393] \"Really? But a locked room\u2026\"\n[1394] \"You seem to have observed less than Jiroku. Itoh goes there every night. Do you think someone would spend time in a room with nothing in it? The chief is looking for another room.\" [1395] \"I see.\" [1396] I felt enlightened. It seemed that everything would be understood by me in a way that was not known to me. [1397] \"But then, the situation becomes even more confusing.\" [1398] \"No, there's a key point here.\" [1399] Surprisingly, Mutsu refuted my opinion. He sat down on the sofa and continued, \"It's a very important key, but it's one-sided.\" [1400] \"Is it the key to the locked room?\" [1401] When I asked, I saw Mukai's impatient expression. [1402] \"Since you said so, it means you didn't notice it either.\" [1403] \"What do you mean?\" [1404] It seems that it's not about questioning my ability anymore. I always have a feeling that Mukai is intentionally belittling me, and the object of belittlement is not at the level of \"humanity\", but it's close. The most important thing is that it's Mukai, which makes me a little angry. [1405] \"How the room was made, why the head and feet were cut off... you and Inspector Jushi Village think about these things, don't you?\" [1406] \"Yes, aren't these major issues?\" [1407] \"They're trivial matters! While they may show the overall picture of the event, they are still only superficial phenomena. Relying on these alone would be impossible to catch the killer lurking in the depths.\" [1408] Mochizuki was usually persuasive, but this statement didn't sound like something he would say. [1409] \"Why?\" [1410] \"Because this is the city of Crow Castle.\" [1411] \"...\"\n[1412] Dangerously, his words held a strange persuasiveness. It seemed like they could explain everything with just one sentence. [1413] \"But how am I supposed to understand that?\" [1414] \"There's no specific reason. The atmosphere here is the bottleneck, the 'key'.\" [1415] \"So, Detective Juzu is unable to catch the killer, then?\" [1416] Mutsuji nodded. \"As a product of modern civilization, his abilities are too weak.\" [1417] A decisive statement. At the same time, it meant that Mutsuji would be confronting everything. [1418] \"So, what should we do?\" [1419] \"Observe with your eyes and think.\" [1420] Mutsuji spoke in a vague manner, like a rhetorician. [1421] \"Your words are too abstract.\" [1422] \"Is that so? Then let me explain it in a way you can understand.\" [1423] \"Thank you very much.\" [1424] Mutoh looked up at the sky, his gaze wandering. [1425] \"For example, isn't the door of 'Hell's Gate' carved with a crucified Jesus carrying a cross? However, that carving does not represent the savior of humanity, Christ, but rather a suffering human being distorted by pain.\" [1426] \"A human?\" [1427] \"Indeed, the one who could not bear the burden of God, who abandoned everything. As I mentioned before, the painting represents the denial of faith. No, it would be more accurate to say the denial of righteousness. Many people in the world support this view.\" [1428] \"However, the room is called 'Hell's Gate,' which means they acknowledge this point.\" [1429] \"Don't forget that hell is also the domain of God. The problem lies in another phenomenon. It is the salvation message written by the 'invisible hand of God' in the center of the room. People in dire straits are constantly waiting for the arrival of the savior, and that text is God's response. Pure expectation of God is equivalent to faith. In other words, the inside and outside of the same room are opposed in thought. I don't understand the meaning of this paradox and the purpose of it.\" [1430] \"Which side is the killer's intention?\" [1431] \"Of course, it is the other side of Nirvana.\" [1432] I am aware of the chaotic atmosphere in this place and understand what Mizuki wants to say but doesn't say. However, this is a defiance of the phenomenon. [1433] \"But the killer is a human, so his thinking should be very human, shouldn't it?\" [1434] \"No, maybe the killer is not the kind of 'human' you are talking about.\" [1435] \"So, is it in that empty room that the killer performed rituals like the Fire Worship ceremony? At the center of the circle?\" [1436] \"Maybe it was a spirit that cursed him to death.\" [1437] Mutsu seemed serious. [1438] Kanou came to our room not long after that. [1439] \"Oh, it's Kanou-san,\" said Mutsu, who seemed to have transformed into a completely different person, welcoming Kanou into the room. The previous shadows had disappeared. [1440] Kanou seemed to be visiting for the second time, but still appeared reserved. Even as an observer, I couldn't help but think, \"Come on, you're the client. Why don't you act more like it?\" As for Mutsu, it seemed he enjoyed maintaining this status quo. I could tell he was enjoying the emotional change. It was one of his specialties. [1441] Just like yesterday, Kaneko spoke of trivial matters, delaying the reveal of his purpose. An impatient Mukai suddenly pointed to the core issue: \"So, you're worried about your daughter, right?\" [1442] Kaneko's head shot up, akin to Mary Magdalene encountering the resurrected Jesus. Although it may sound exaggerated, the impact on him seemed similar. He gazed at Mukai, as if to say, \"How did you know?\" Neither had mentioned Mizuho before, so his surprise was understandable. [1443] Mukai simply grinned, offering no explanation. [1444] \"Did I get it right?\" [1445] \"Yes.\" [1446] Muto began to ramble on. He appeared respectful but actually arrogant, and his words and actions exuded more vigor than tenderness. Unconsciously, Sugiyama revealed his true intentions - or, in other words, the purpose of this visit. [1447] \"I believe you've already done your research. Mizuho is a bastard. Mizuho's mother left me during her pregnancy... and I didn't know about it until six months ago.\" [1448] He began to recount his story in a calm manner, just like I heard in the entrance of the courtyard earlier. It was a familiar tale of emotional tragedy. [1449] Sugiyama clasped his hands in front of him, as if confessing to a priest. [1450] \"I intended to claim Mizuho, but her mother had already passed away. I heard she suffered greatly to raise Mizuho.\" [1451] Sugata took a breath. Perhaps due to the surge of emotions, his voice became disorganized. [1452] \"Is it because of the difference in social status that you opposed the marriage?\" [1453] \"The origin of the Inari family is also strange, even more suspicious than the Borgia family.\" Sugata smiled sarcastically, \"So if it's just a matter of social status, there is still a way to think about it...\"\n[1454] \"So the problem is with the other party?\" [1455] Sugata hesitated for a moment before saying, \"Have you investigated Mistuho's mother's name?\" [1456] \"We haven't reached that point yet.\" Muto shrugged and shook his head.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:28 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1] First Part\n[2] Crows,\n[3] Fly home! [4] Tell your master all you have seen,\n[5] On the Rhine's banks! [6] Fly past Brunhild's cliffs,\n[7] Let Loge return to Valhalla's halls! [8] For the end of the gods is near,\n[9] So -\n[10] I will cast this torch into Valhalla's gleaming tower. [11] Richard Wagner's \"Twilight of the Gods\"\n[12] Chapter 1: The Dawn\n[13] 1\n[14] The next day, we went to the Inari Shrine. [15] Boring scenery. [16] The asphalt road, with no branching off, twisted and turned like a snake that had fainted from pain. The forests on both sides, with their subtle elegance, reminded one of Dutch landscape paintings. The setting sun gently swayed the contours of the leaves, casting its light on the car's hood. [17] \"It's like a landlocked island,\" Mutsuji's exaggeration was not entirely unfounded, as the scenery outside the window had remained unchanged for the past few minutes, like a frozen frame from a video. [18] The lush green oaks that must have been beautiful and verdant in the summer turned dull and dreary as soon as winter arrived, with only shades of dark gray and deep brown in sight, further emphasizing the monotony of the scenery. [19] \"Mom used to tell me that people shouldn't waste time,\" Mutsuji suppressed a yawn and murmured from the passenger seat. [20] Since earlier, the car radio had been broadcasting a ten-kilometer traffic jam on the Osaka Expressway. Traveling through this desolate area, the normally helpful traffic information now sounded like nothing more than an irritating noise. [21] \"Although it's boring to be stuck in traffic, at least there's a clear object to vent anger against, which is quite good.\" [22] \"Is that what your mother said?\" [23] \"Yes,\" Muto answered listlessly. [24] My mother never taught me that, so I couldn't ask any further. More importantly, saying something meaningless when there's no meaning doesn't cancel anything out. Muto also remained silent, switching the radio channels. [25] At the northern end of the Kyoto Basin lies a place called \"Kama,\" and further north is Mount Danwa, which directly pierces the Sea of Japan. The residence of the Miura family is located there. The natural scenery, untouched by the hustle and bustle of the times, spreads out in all directions, with countless sights to behold. If one were to seek to retire and become a hermit, this place would be the most suitable. [26] However, from a geographical perspective, it is only a one and a half hour drive from the city, and there is not much difference from the suburban new residential areas. Some company employees now spend two or three hours commuting, so this place might be a decent location. The environment is also favored by nature, and the land prices are particularly cheap. [27] After a while, we arrived at a bright place. The previously unruly branches were neatly trimmed, indicating that we had entered the territory of the Matsuzaki family. [28] There was a building resembling a door in front of us. The door was in a half-collapsed state, with a Kama-style iron gun tip pointing upwards, forming an ellipse, a rare structure in Japan. The paint had peeled off, and it seemed that the owner had no intention of repairing it. [29] After passing through the door, we walked along a 500-meter-long tree-lined avenue. The trees, which are often seen in French films with only their tops covered in leaves, seemed to have been neglected and were half withered and weak. [30] I slowed down in the tree-lined avenue and drove slowly. [31] \"That's it,\" said Kumagai, pointing with his hand. [32] The sandy road ended at a small arc at the fountain in the distance, and our destination, the Inkyou Castle, loomed in front of us. [33] The Inkyou Castle had a dark overall tone, resembling a giant old man squatting there. [34] The Gothic-style mansion was built very solidly. The doorway on the side of the rough brick wall formed a semicircle, and the windows on the front were covered by thick iron shutters, with a pattern engraved on the protruding wall in front, which was worn out and difficult to see. It was probably a coat of arms or something similar. [35] The blue roof sloped down from the center of the mansion, with two conical roofs of the same light blue color rising from each side, pointing upwards, forming a \"mountain\" shape. The three spires, symbolizing the sacred number \"3\", pierced the clouds and firmly rooted themselves to the ground. [36] Although the Sparrow Castle was not as meticulously crafted as King Ludwig's Neuschwanstein Castle, it still evoked the feeling of viewing medieval castle ruins scattered along the Rhine River. [37] \"This is the Sparrow Castle,\" I exclaimed involuntarily. I had heard of it before, but this was the first time I had seen it. The color of the roofs also seemed to resemble a sparrow ready to take flight. The two towers at the ends were like its flapping wings. Existing in a place just an hour and a half from the ancient capital of Kyoto, such a building... I stood there, feeling the cultural shock, for a long time. [38] \"It's quite romantic,\" Mutsuji said, uninterested. [39] \"You mean 'low-level interest', right?\" [40] \"It's said that the name 'Sparrow Castle' comes from the Italian poet Luigi's seventeenth-century prose poem 'The Night of the Sparrow'. In the poem, the sparrow represents the personification of death, coming to take the souls of children at dawn.\" [41] \"Children?\" [42] \" 'At the dawn of the raven's cry, on the south wind, the death messenger arrives; at the dawn of the sleeping village, on the south wind, he appears to seek the child. His scythe... ' That's the gist of it,\" Mutsuji stopped reciting, and added, \"However, Luigi Righetto himself was sentenced to death for murdering adult women.\" [43] \"How ominous,\" I thought, looking at Mutsuji. I wondered if it was true. A sense of unease that had been hidden before swept over me. [44] \"Oh, it's a red rose,\" he exclaimed, pointing out the window of the car. \"Everything was prepared for this.\" [45] The fountain in front of Hohenwerfen Castle imitates the Medici Garden in Italy, with water overflowing from it, calm and peaceful, perfectly blending with the surrounding atmosphere. The circular rim has several cracks, and the reddish-brown bricks add a somber mood. [46] Loneliness and tranquility... If expressed in Japanese, my current state of mind would be like the verses left by Basho when he died. [47] However, unlike the introspective interest of the builder, Mutsuji and I were filled with anxiety about how the future would unfold and \"expectation.\" Such a posture, although clich\u00e9d, is not bad. [48] Mutsuji pointed to several cars parked by the pool. The body was painted in a practical two-tone paint, completely unrelated to loneliness and quietude, and a symbol was carefully printed on the surface - Kyoto Prefectural Police. The last one was a gray box-shaped car of the forensic department. [49] \"Is something wrong?\" [50] \"Of course, you should think so.\" Mutsuji seemed to be used to it, with a calm and composed expression. [51]\"A murder case?\" [52]\"Probably. We may have arrived a step too late. Detectives are always a move behind.\" [53]I park my car behind the police cars. It seems that no one is outside. [54]Mukumizu holds his hat in one hand and steps out of the car, walking briskly forward. He is delighted with this unoriginal entrance, despite his repeated performances. [55]\"Let's meet with Mr. Itou first.\" [56] \"Even with the tea leaves standing upright this morning, I still have a bad feeling.\" [57] \"Well, you never know. You might have just jinxed it by saying that.\" [58] \"Really?\" [59] Mochizuki chuckled again. \"It's all about reversing cause and effect, isn't it?\" [60] \"You mean Mr. Itou?\" [61] \"Who knows?\" [62] The worst scenario was unfolding in my mind, but if it weren't, Mutsu's appearance would have no meaning. I walked towards the mansion with a complicated mood. [63] \"Oh, it's Mutsu-san! And Hoshimo-san is here too!\" [64] At the front of the porch stood a man in a marble arch. It was he who called out to us - Detective Tsubaki. Detective Tsubaki was wearing a trench coat, his only decent piece of clothing. He spread his hands wide, showing surprise. Detective Tsubaki was already forty, but he had a childlike face when he smiled. [65] Detective Tsubaki was a rare person who was straightforward in his emotions. Of course, when dealing with cases, he would put on a serious face, but otherwise, he was a likable neighborhood uncle figure. It was all thanks to Detective Tsubaki's influence that a commoner like Mutsu was able to thrive in the police force. [66] \"Detective Joujiroku, it's been a while.\" Mukae takes off his hat and nods slightly. [67] \"I haven't seen you since the arson case. It's been three and a half months.\" [68] \"That's right.\" [69] The two look at each other, as if celebrating their long-awaited reunion. [70] \"Well, Detective Joujiroku from the investigation department has arrived, so it must be a murder case.\" [71] \"You're as informed as ever, almost like a walking encyclopedia. I don't recall telling you about this...\"\n[72] Detective Jiro spoke while observing Shizuku's face, smiling but with a sharp gaze. [73] \"I came here on behalf of Mr. Mikage Itou's request.\" [74] The detective looked even more surprised as he stared at Shizuku. It seemed he was genuinely shocked, but he nodded in understanding and reached into his pocket. [75] \"Well, it looks like you're in too deep now.\" [76] \"Are you talking about the case?\" Kumotsubashi smiled, more composed than before. [77] \"Luckily, not the coffin. But if you say 'in too deep to get out,' is the victim Mr. Itou?\" [78] Jingu looked at Kumotsubashi with a \"you really get straight to the point\" expression and whispered, \"It seems so. I just arrived. The first to arrive were Mr. Horikawa and his team.\" [79] \"Well, we might as well go take a look then?\" [80] \"Sure,\" Jingu nodded proudly. \"Do I have a choice?\" [81] \"You're right.\" [82] The dark oak door creaked loudly as it opened inward. [83] A ray of light entered the room. Gradually, the bright lines grew thicker, as if predicting the future events. [84] I became anxious, but Muto whispered, \"We've taken the first step.\" [85] The ceiling of the entrance hall was very high, extending up to the third floor, forming a stairwell, and a golden chandelier hung from the semicircular canopy. [86] The searchers were likely all on the scene, but the spacious hall was empty and silent, just like the outdoors beyond the door. [87] The floor was covered with a deep red carpet that stretched across the entire room. It appeared to be a high-quality material, with a thickness measured in centimeters, and it provided a noticeable bounce when stepped on. [88] The staircase stood in the center of the hall, with a wide entrance and a turn leading up to the second floor. Like the National Diet Building, the staircase was also covered with a red carpet. In addition to the carpet on the floor and staircase, the wallpaper and color of the furniture were all in a uniform red hue. There seemed to be subtle differences in color, perhaps intentionally, which gave the hall a feeling of twilight even though it was daytime. [89] There were two passages on either side of the hall, likely leading to the towers. They were dark and seemed to be swallowing up the light. [90] \"The raven's stomach is red, isn't it strange? It's like a redshift... I feel dizzy.\" [91] However, Mutsu did not seem to be listening to me. He hung his cherished hat on the coat rack by the door, then suddenly changed his mind and put it back on his head again: \"What an exquisite and ancient taste, Hagino-san. This may turn out to be more interesting than we initially thought.\" [92] His face was adorned with an enigmatic smile. [93] Later, Mutsu mentioned \"Eryx's Vessel,\" \"Lacan's Holy Grail,\" and \"Al-Rus' Mermaid,\" praising them highly. I was not familiar with these terms as I had no interest in antiquities. However, the few statues and ornaments from prehistoric times to the 15th and 16th centuries displayed on the wall shelves still fascinated me, a layman. This place was like a museum of ancient art, and the artifacts on display were probably acquired at great cost. [94] \"I don't want this case to turn into the kind you like,\" the inspector sighed, switching to a work mode expression. [95] \"You're getting timid, Inspector. Are you almost ready to join the old-timers' ranks?\" Mutsu grinned mischievously. [96] Tsujimura belongs to the generation commonly referred to as the wartime generation. [97] \"Who said that! I've been busy with trivial matters and haven't had time to think about it. I've been so busy that I've wanted to take a week off.\" [98] Upon closer inspection, the inspector's head was already peppered with white hairs. [99] \"And the increase in the crime-solving rate can enhance the reputation of the police, can't it? Criminal investigation has nothing to do with conscience; it's just a matter of a livelihood.\" The inspector put the purple crystal mermaid statue back in its place and strutted towards the stairs. [100] \"Inspector Tsujimura, have you noticed this?\" [101] The word \"decorations\" referred to the armor standing on either side of the stairs. They were like knights standing guard, despite not being the legendary figures of \"humph\" and \"ha.\" [102] From the simple outline, it can be estimated to be a work from the Italian Renaissance period. Unlike the pillars, the armor seemed to have been carefully maintained, with the smooth curves emanating a silvery glow. Although they were a pair, there were subtle differences in their design. Whether intentional or due to technical limitations, the slight asymmetry saved them from a purely inorganic feel. [103] The chief pointed to the right armor. [104] \"It's a bit different. Look, both are wearing a Vescomti-style helmet, and the right shoulder is covered by an unusually large shoulder plate. The legs are of a multi-constructed design, and the overall appearance is quite peculiar. However, I feel that the engraved lines on the right one are a bit finer. The chest plate has a linear and rounded surface covered with lily patterns, which is the same as the left one. \" The chief showed off his knowledge, \"These are all works of goldsmiths. From the precision of the engraving and the state of preservation, they are likely decorative items and belong to the later style.\" [105] \"What's the matter with that?\" [106] \"Don't you find it strange? The gauntlets, gun shields, and elbow protectors are all there, but there are no iron boots on the right armor. None at all!\" [107] The left armor's hands and feet are tied to steel supports, while the iron boots of the right armor are nowhere to be found, with the iron pipes used for fixing them exposed. [108] Indeed, as Mutojin said, it was very unnatural. [109] \"Isn't it because it wasn't there from the beginning?\" Despite his words, Tsubokura's interest was undeniable. Mutojin's trivial words often turned out to have extraordinary significance, and Tsubokura had seen enough of this to react as he did. [110] \"Is this what you call breaking the balance? But as paired decorative items, it's a bit out of balance. In addition, there are traces of the buckles being unfastened on the inside of the leg armor.\" [111] Mukai pointed to the inside of the multi-layered leg protection to show us. There was a piece of tanned leather with hooks that was used to install the iron boots. [112] \"I know. But what does that mean?\" [113] \"And then what?\" [114] Mukai shrugged his shoulders as usual: \"Let's stay in the stage of questioning for now, as we will eventually understand the meaning.\" [115] \"But it has to be meaningful, you know. You always do this, finding some strange situation and then only saying vague things.\" The police chief tapped the armor with his finger, making a sharp \"clang\" sound. [116] \"But I always give a clear answer in the end, don't I?\" [117] \"Is that so... It's still a matter of method, isn't it?\" [118] Juzu village raised his hands in confusion, then ascended the stairs. The stairs were also covered in a carpet of blood color. It was easy to imagine that the whole house would be in this style. The owner seemed to be quite an obsessive person. [119] Starting from the landing of the stairs, the stairs divided to the side walls on both sides, leading to the second floor separately. [120] \"It's better to have more puzzles. The more contradictions there are, the more paths there are to the truth.\" [121] \"Just don't get too tangled up.\" Jichi Village grumbled sarcastically. With his single-minded nature, it was inevitable that he would be troubled and slow when it came to \"parallel progress.\" [122] \"It's a matter of choosing how to proceed, right? By the way, is the scene on the second floor?\" [123] \"But Detective, why don't you go meet the people in this house?\" Detective turned to lean on the railing and asked. [124] \"Since I know the victim is my client, I can only go with the flow from here on out. It's not too late to see the people of the Kumihara family later, right?\" [125] \"And I am the source of the current?\" [126] \"You are the lucky god!\" [127] \"It's better to be Bacchus,\" said the drinker Tsujimura. [128] \"Of course, that's just to comfort you.\" [129] \"I know, I know!\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 9:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:28 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"So, staying out overnight is a common occurrence?\" [287] \"Yes. However, I didn't know he had already returned.\" [288] Arai sat down opposite Tsubaki. The scent of incense filled the air. [289] \"What do you mean?\" [290] \"Last night, he called here and said he would stay in the city for the night.\" [291] \"From the city... really?\" [292] \"Ask Yoshino. She answered the phone.\" [293] \"Yoshino? Ah, the housekeeper?\" [294] \"Of course.\" [295] Arai nodded lazily, his posture resembling that of a mummy with its neck tilted. [296] \"But, Tsutsuji... \"\n[297] \"Tsutsuji is about 100 kilometers away from here. Even by car, it would take half a day to cross the Danbo Plateau.\" [298] \"Suzuki-san crossed his arms and glanced at Tsukimi. Tsukimi, however, seemed uninterested and simply swayed his head slightly.\" [299] \"By the way, Mr. Tsukimi-san.\" [300] \"What?\" [301] \"Do you know where we might find the head of Mr. Itou?\" Mukizuki looked intently at the old man, his lips slightly loosening. [302] Inaba didn't seem too surprised, raising his eyebrows a few times before saying, \"Why are you asking me? Do you think I'm the killer?\" [303] \"Of course not. You should know that yourself. However, the killer seems to want us to find the head. So, where would be the most effective place? I think asking you would help us understand.\" [304] \"Ah, I see. You're right.\" [305] Inaba's face showed admiration, and he removed his reading glasses, revealing his gray eyes that glimmered faintly. [306] \"\u2026That would be the entrance to hell.\" [307] \"Hell's entrance?\" [308] \"I mean 'door,' but it's actually the name of the room. That's probably where it is, if your theory is correct.\" Akuang smiled. [309] Woodside returned the smile. [310] In the red-dominated entrance hall, besides the central staircase, there are three other paths leading to different directions. One goes straight to the dining room and courtyard beside the staircase, while the other two branch off to the