en if it's to find a lost dog. It is not surprising that we can say that a detective is not a job but a hobby. [387] Moreover, the content of the cases is almost trivial disputes among the upper class, which is one of the reasons why Wooden Bridge is reluctant to take them. [388] In fact, Wooden Bridge once took a case hastily, and suffered a setback. [389] \"Staying away from such places is bound to bring bad luck.\" [390] From then on, this became his catchphrase. [391] However, the content of this letter seems to suit Mochizuki's taste, which is quite rare. He wore a strange smile - even a bit flirtatious...\n[392] \"Take a look at the letter, Hagio-san.\" [393] Mochizuki seems to want me to read the letter in his hand first. [394] The details of how I met Mochizuki are omitted here (if you want to know, please wait for Mochizuki's autobiography, which will soon be published). In short, the newly emerged mystery novelist - me, and my ideal protagonist in the novel - Mochizuki, have had a common interest in the field of criminal reasoning since our university days. This kind of closed circle of like-minded people is often referred to as a \"clique.\" After graduation, our relationship has been maintained because both of our professions are not bound by time. [395] So, on this day, I came to Mochizuki's place to play - perhaps I could even get some inspiration for my writing...\n[396] \"Is it okay?\" I asked politely. [397] \"Of course. It doesn't look like a love letter.\" [398] His indifferent demeanor doesn't seem to suggest that it's a love letter. [399] The envelope contains only a note sheet. It is of high quality, with a light yellow color and a delicate floral pattern in the corner, and a watermark of the same flower in the center. [400] The letter is written with a fountain pen, perhaps due to the use of light ink, the overall handwriting is light. The text arranged randomly on the paper gradually sinks to the right, with spaces between the lines. The writer seems to write quickly, with many white spaces and noticeable strokes. [401] The content was also quite brief, or rather, impolite. [402] The letter contained only one stiffly worded sentence: \"Come to my residence at 10:00 a.m. on December 2nd for consultation.\" [403] Apart from the time and place, there was no other content. Not even a mention of what the consultation was about. It was almost like a draft for a military recruitment notice. [404] \"What's this? It's quite interesting... But this attitude is too high-handed.\" [405] I was taken aback. I had seen many unusual and lengthy complaint letters, but this letter was one of the most peculiar ones I had ever seen. [406] \"It may seem like that at first glance without any prior knowledge. Didn't you see the name of the sender clearly?\" [407] \"Did it say?\" [408] \"It's at the end.\" [409] Indeed, it's quite natural for the envelope of a detective agency to have no name. Because no one wants others to know they are hiring a detective. [410] I looked at the name written at the end, which was a signature that sank to the right and downward - Tomijiro It\u00f4. [411] \"This Ito Kagami... is that...\"\n[412] \"Yes! That's the Ito Kagami. I can't think of any other Ito Kagami.\" [413] In Kyoto, the surname Ito is well-known. Even those outside of Kyoto have heard of the Ito Group. The headquarters of the Ito Group is located in Osaka and it is a large corporation with assets worth hundreds of billions of yen. [414] The history of the company is not well-known, but it is said that the group started with a textile company called \"Ito Silk Factory\" which was established in the Meiji era. After that, the company followed the trend of \"Rich Country, Strong Army, Promote Industry\" and developed successfully, gradually entering the mechanical manufacturing industry. Then, it grew into a large zaibatsu during the late Meiji and early Showa periods due to the expansion of military needs. Some say it was because of